      "notes": "The <strong>upper limit of the Harcourt traditional pedigree</strong>. Bernard appears as a major figure in <strong>Dudo of Saint-Quentin's c. 1015 history of the early Norman dukes</strong>, where he is described as a Danish-origin warrior of Rouen, councillor to William Longsword, and <strong>co-regent of Normandy during Richard I's minority</strong> after William's assassination in 942. The 17th-century French genealogist <strong>Gilles-André de La Roque</strong> (in his 1662 <em>Histoire généalogique de la maison de Harcourt</em>) made Bernard the patriarch of the d'Harcourt family — and from him every printed Harcourt and Beauchamp pedigree since has traced its origin.<br><br>\n<strong>The modern scholarly view, however, is skeptical:</strong> Leah Shopkow (<em>History and Community: Norman Historical Writing</em>, 1997) argues Bernard is likely a Dudo literary invention — a stock \"wise Danish counsellor\"; the contemporary chronicler <strong>Flodoard of Reims</strong>, writing in detail through the same period, never once mentions him; <strong>Charles Cawley's FMG Medieval Lands</strong> explicitly rejects the claim that Bernard was father of Torf le Riche (Torfin Karl), calling La Roque's \"Preuves\" undated and unverifiable. The honest position: <em>Bernard the Dane may have been real but his connection to the Harcourt and Beauchamp lines is a 17th-century reconstruction, not history.</em><br><br>\n<strong>This is where the chain genuinely ends.</strong> Going beyond Bernard — to Hastein, Rollo, Rognvald Eysteinsson, or further Viking ancestors — is not supported by any Harcourt or Beauchamp tradition; those names appear in unrelated saga material or pure 18th-19th c. genealogical fabrication. The 33-generation gap from Kip to Bernard (if any of this holds) represents <em>about 1,150 years of unbroken family memory</em>, half of it now provably reconstructed rather than recorded.",
