      "rel": "speculative 29th-great-grandfather (father of Hugh per family tradition)",
      "notes": "Identified in the Geneanet tree as Hugh's father. Geneanet places his birthplace at <em>Beauchamp, Calvados</em> — but the regional Norman authority <strong>Wikimanche</strong> and 19th-century herald J.R. Planché both place the family seat at <strong>Beauchamps in La Manche</strong> (canton La Haye-Pesnel, in the Avranchin, between Avranches and Granville), <em>not</em> in the Bessin near Bayeux. The Manche commune is described by Wikimanche as <em>le berceau anglo-normand des Beauchamp devenus comtes de Warwick et de Worcester</em> — the Anglo-Norman cradle of the Beauchamps who became Earls of Warwick and Worcester; 1066.co.nz adds the conjecture that the family was a cadet branch of the <strong>Barons of Saint-Denis-le-Gast</strong>. The 1066 death date aligns with the Norman Conquest year; family tradition holds that Robert may have died at or around Hastings, leaving the young Hugh to come into his English inheritance under William the Conqueror. <strong>Modern peer-reviewed scholarship (Mason, Keats-Rohan, FMG Medieval Lands) explicitly states Hugh's parentage is undocumented in surviving charters</strong> — and no Beauchamp is among the Proven Companions of William the Conqueror in primary sources. This Robert is therefore <em>speculative family tradition rather than primary-source history</em>. Under the competing <strong>Wikimanche identification</strong> (which conflates Hugues I de Beauchamps with our Hugh of Bedford as one person), no separate Robert generation is needed — Hugh himself is the Hastings combatant. The Robert generation is shown here only because the Geneanet family tradition records it; the regional Norman tradition does not.",
