      "id": "hugh",
      "nm": "Hugh / Hugues I de Beauchamp",
      "ep": "Seigneur de Beauchamps · 1st Feudal Baron of Bedford",
      "dt": "c. 1040 – after 1100",
      "rel": "28th-great-grandfather",
      "notes": "Norman tenant-in-chief; held <strong>43 manors in Bedfordshire</strong> at the Domesday survey of 1086 plus holdings in Buckinghamshire and Hertfordshire. Held Bedford Castle. Witnessed a charter of William the Conqueror as \"sheriff\" between 1070 and 1089. The proposed link from Hugh's Bedford line to the Worcestershire Beauchamps (next generation) is traditional from Sir William Dugdale's seventeenth-century pedigree but not documented in contemporary charters. <em>Marked as traditional accordingly.</em>\n<p><strong>Surname etymology.</strong> <em>Beauchamp</em> derives from the Old French <em>Bel-Champ</em> — \"fine or lovely field\" — Latinised in medieval English records as <em>de Bello Campo</em>. Common variant spellings: Beecham, Beacham.</p>\n<p><strong>The Norman seat: Beauchamps in La Manche.</strong> Multiple sources — the regional Norman authority <strong>Wikimanche</strong>, 19th-century herald J.R. Planché in <em>Companions of the Conqueror</em>, and the Battle Abbey Roll commentary at 1066.co.nz — agree that the family's pre-Conquest seat was the commune of <strong>Beauchamps in La Manche</strong> (canton La Haye-Pesnel, dépt Manche, in the Avranchin region between Avranches and Granville), <em>not</em> Beauchamps-en-Bessin near Bayeux as some online genealogies state. Wikimanche calls this commune <em>\"le berceau anglo-normand des Beauchamp devenus comtes de Warwick et de Worcester\"</em> — the Anglo-Norman cradle of the Beauchamps who became Earls of Warwick and Worcester. 1066.co.nz adds the conjecture that the family was a cadet branch of the <strong>Barons of Saint-Denis-le-Gast</strong>, the regional baronial house. The family was likely <em>minor-to-middling Norman landed knights</em> rather than first-rank ducal magnates: no Beauchamp appears in any surviving Norman ducal charter before 1066, and no Beauchamp is among the ~15 \"Proven Companions of William the Conqueror\" who can be securely identified at Hastings from contemporary sources. The Battle Abbey Roll and Falaise Roll, both of which list a \"Beauchampe,\" are 14th-century compilations not contemporary 1066 musters.</p>\n<p><strong>Three competing reconstructions of his identity.</strong> The historical record around Hugh's exact identity is contested — three traditions are worth knowing:</p>\n<ul style=\"margin:8px 0 12px 24px; font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.55;\">\n<li><strong>Wikimanche / regional Norman tradition</strong> — Hugh is the same person as <strong>Hugues I, seigneur de Beauchamps</strong> in La Manche; born <strong>c. 1040</strong>; accompanied William the Conqueror at Hastings in 1066 (about age 26); identified with Domesday's \"Hugo de Belcamp\" (1086); died after 1100. <em>One person, fighting age at Hastings.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Cawley / FMG / peer-reviewed academic</strong> — Hugh born <strong>c. 1050-1060</strong>, deduced from his absence from any William I charter (he comes of age only late in William's reign; first documented under William II Rufus 1087-1100). Under this reading he was <strong>6-16 at Hastings</strong> and almost certainly not a combatant. His parentage is undocumented in surviving primary sources.</li>\n<li><strong>Geneanet \"irishwhiskey\" tradition</strong> — there are two generations: a father <strong>Robert de Beauchamp</strong> (b. c. 1020, d. c. 1066 — possibly at or around Hastings) and his son <strong>Hugh</strong> (b. c. 1040). Under this reading, the Conquest-era combatant is Robert, who dies in 1066, and the young Hugh inherits the English lands. <em>Speculative — no primary source corroborates Robert as a separate person.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Wikimanche identification has the merit of <em>simplicity</em> (one person, doing all the documented deeds); the Cawley dating is more rigorous; the Geneanet two-generation tradition explains the 1066 death date attributed to Robert but adds an undocumented generation. This tree shows the Geneanet structure (Robert as Hugh's speculative father) for breadth, but the Wikimanche identification — <strong>Hugues I, seigneur de Beauchamps</strong> = our Hugh — is the regional Norman authority's view.</p>\n<p>Hugh's 47 manors in Domesday 1086 reflect not pre-Conquest baronial rank but a post-Conquest reward — and probably his marriage to <strong>Matilda Taillebois</strong>, almost certainly daughter and heiress of Ralph Taillebois (sheriff of Bucks before Hugh, who married Hugh into a major Norman tenancy).</p>\n<p><strong>Family arms (senior Earls of Warwick branch).</strong> Blazon: <em>Gules, a fesse between six cross crosslets or</em>. Crest: <em>Out of a ducal coronet or, a swan's head gules</em>. Motto: <strong>Verité sans peur</strong> — \"Truth without fear.\" (Source: H.R.C. Family Name History certificate, supplied by the family.)</p>\n<p><strong>Bayeux Tapestry tradition.</strong> Family memory holds that one of the unnamed Norman knights depicted around William the Conqueror in the Bayeux Tapestry may represent Hugh — characterised as a mid-level commander with authority rather than a \"right-hand man.\" The Tapestry names William's chief lieutenants (William fitz Osbern, Bishop Odo, Robert of Mortain) but leaves most of the supporting knights anonymous. There is no surviving documentary evidence that confirms or denies a Hugh identification; the tradition lives on the strength of the family knowing he was at the Conquest.</p>\n<p><em>A note on names.</em> Family memory sometimes fuses Hugh I (the Domesday tenant-in-chief, d. c. 1100) with his grandson <strong>Hugh II of Bedford</strong> (d. c. 1141, son of Simon). Charters distinguish them: it's Hugh I who held the 43 Bedfordshire manors at Domesday, and Hugh II who was holding Bedford Castle through the Anarchy.</p>\n<p><strong>Modern scholarship.</strong> Per Emma Mason's ODNB entry on Walter de Beauchamp, K.S.B. Keats-Rohan's <em>Domesday Descendants</em>, and the FMG <em>Medieval Lands</em> project: Hugh's only documented children are <strong>Simon</strong> (d. c. 1137) and <strong>Robert</strong> (d. before 1130). The 17th-century Dugdale reconstruction that places <strong>Walter de Beauchamp of Elmley</strong> as Hugh's third son is <em>not corroborated by contemporary charters</em>; Walter's parentage is unknown in surviving records. The Bedford and Elmley/Warwick branches of the Beauchamps may in fact be distinct families. This tree preserves the traditional Dugdale descent for continuity with the Lorraine Shoemaker compilation, but the join between Hugh and Walter is <em>genealogically unproven</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>The Crusader cousin.</strong> Through Hugh's documented son Robert → grandson Payn de Beauchamp (m. Rohese de Vere, founder of Chicksand Priory) → great-grandson Simon (d. 1207) ran a cadet line that included <strong>Hugh de Beauchamp of Eaton Bray</strong>, who took the cross in 1186, sailed for the Holy Land with <strong>Roger de Mowbray</strong>, and was <strong>killed at the Battle of Hattin on 4 July 1187</strong> — the catastrophic Crusader defeat that triggered the Third Crusade. His wife Philippa de Trailly made a recorded pious benefaction at St. Neots Priory at his departure. (Source: G.H. Fowler, \"The Beauchamps, Barons of Eaton,\" <em>Beds. Hist. Rec. Soc.</em> 2, 1914; Christopher Tyerman, <em>England and the Crusades</em>, p. 162; the Independent Crusaders Project, Fordham.)</p>",
      "sources": "Domesday Book (1086); Cokayne, <em>Complete Peerage</em> vol II (Beauchamp of Bedford); Dugdale, <em>Baronage</em> (1675); modern: Emma Mason in <em>Oxford DNB</em> (Walter de Beauchamp); K.S.B. Keats-Rohan, <em>Domesday Descendants</em>; FMG <em>Medieval Lands</em>, ENGLISH NOBILITY MEDIEVAL3 (Charles Cawley); J.R. Planché, <em>Companions of the Conqueror</em>; G.H. Fowler in <em>Bedfordshire Historical Record Society</em> vol. 2 (1914); Christopher Tyerman, <em>England and the Crusades</em>; the Independent Crusaders Project, Fordham.",
