      "notes": "Born <strong>c. 1065</strong>, died between <strong>1130 and 1133</strong> (last appears in the 1130 Pipe Roll). One of <em>Henry I's \"new men\"</em> — promoted from obscurity by royal favor rather than great hereditary descent. His parentage is not documented in contemporary records: the traditional 17th-century reconstruction placing him as a son of Hugh de Beauchamp of Bedford is <em>not corroborated</em> by Mason's ODNB study, Keats-Rohan, or the FMG project. Inherited the Worcestershire estates and the hereditary <strong>Sheriffdom of Worcestershire</strong> through his wife Emeline — but the \"inheritance\" was actually a royal grant after her brother <strong>Roger d'Abetot was exiled by Henry I for murdering a royal servant</strong>. Walter held the shrievalty <strong>c. 1114–1130</strong>; was a <strong>royal dispenser</strong> (precursor to butler) and probably royal forester of Worcestershire. Witnessed royal charters 1108–1111. His widow Adeliza's dower lands were confirmed to him by Henry I 1123-29. A long-running land dispute with the Marmion family over Urse's brother Robert's lands ran into the late 12th century. Three sons: <strong>William</strong> (heir, ancestor of the Earls of Warwick), <strong>Stephen</strong>, and <strong>Walter II</strong>. <strong>Elmley Castle</strong> would remain the principal Beauchamp residence for 158 years.",
