      "notes": "Orphaned at age five. His wardship and marriage were sold by the crown — first to William de Braose, then for the staggering sum of <strong>3,000 marks</strong> to Roger Mortimer of Wigmore, who married him to his daughter Joan. Held the shrievalty during the First Barons' War; <strong>joined Prince Louis of France's invasion in May 1216</strong>, was <em>excommunicated, lost his lands, then reconciled with Henry III</em> after King John's death in October 1216. Restored as Sheriff of Worcestershire and an itinerant justice.\n<p><strong>Magna Carta, 1216.</strong> The uploaded book photos show the opening of Henry III's 1216 reissue naming <strong>Walter de Beauchamp</strong> among the noblemen whose advice/support stood behind the charter. That belongs here: this is the same Walter of Elmley, appearing in the public settlement immediately after King John's death and Henry III's accession.</p>\n<p><em>Family-memory framing.</em> Family tradition remembers him as the son who was \"disgraced and later reinstated after a successful coup.\" That reads neatly onto the documented arc: in 1216 he briefly sided with the rebel/Louis party, was excommunicated and stripped of estates, then made peace with the minority government after John's death. His appearance with the 1216 reissue is the paper trail of that rehabilitation; by 1217 he was restored to the shrievalty and to royal favour.</p>",
      "sources": "User-uploaded photos IMG_2938.jpeg / IMG_2939.jpeg: printed English text headed \"Magna Carta, 1216,\" p. 789, naming Walter de Beauchamp among the noblemen in the charter's opening address. Cross-check: J. H. Round, \"Beauchamp, Walter de (d.1236),\" Dictionary of National Biography, notes that Walter made peace on Henry III's accession, witnessed the 11 Nov. 1216 charter reissue, and was restored to his shrievalty and castellanship. Library of Congress, Magna Carta: Muse and Mentor, notes Henry III's 1216 reissue through the regency government.",
