      "notes": "Apprenticed for eight years to his future brother-in-law John Doggett as a <strong>mercer</strong>. Made a Freeman of London 9 May 1656. Came to Maryland by 1665. <strong>22 August 1666</strong> Somerset County established by proclamation; Edmund became its first Clerk and Keeper of Records, holding the post 25 years. The folklorist Stith Thompson, his descendant, said his court books were \"<em>a more splendidly kept set of records of court proceedings and deeds cannot, we believe, be found elsewhere during the early colonial period.</em>\" On <strong>30 June 1668</strong>, eleven days after his marriage, purchased <strong>300 acres at the head of the Great Annemessex River — the tract called <em>Contention</em></strong> — from Edmund Dickeson; conveyed it to Sarah as her jointure in 1669. The surviving <strong>Beauchamp House</strong> on this land — built c. 1710-30 by his descendants and enlarged through the 18th and 19th centuries — still stands east of Old Westover-Marion Road, Westover, Somerset County, MD. Listed on the <strong>National Register of Historic Places</strong> 9 August 1984 (NRHP #84001830 / MIHP S-62). Its <strong>Flemish-bond glazed-brick diaper-pattern north gable</strong> is the most ambitious example of patterned brickwork in Somerset County; the original 18th-century paneled hall, fireplace, and stair survive inside. Tradition holds Edmund himself is buried somewhere on Contention. Will dated 10 April 1691; his coat of arms: <em>sable, a chevron argent between three lions</em>. ",
      "sources": "Mary Turpin Layton, <em>D.A.R. Magazine</em> April 1932; Stith Thompson 1954; Torrence, <em>Old Somerset</em> (1935); Maryland State Archives Somerset Co. records; Maryland Historical Trust S-62 architectural survey file (1983, by Paul Baker Touart); NRHP nomination form #84001830 (certified 5 July 1984).",
