      "rel": "2nd-great-grandfather",
      "notes": "Son of John Gaddis and Chloe Fox — <strong>their fifth child</strong>. Born <strong>15 January 1834</strong>. While his parents stayed in Delaware County NY, Lucas was the generation that moved west — to <strong>Wataga, Knox County, Illinois</strong>. Married twice and fathered <strong>13 children total</strong>: first to <strong>Elnora Marinda Carley</strong> (b. 10 May 1847, d. 4 Jun 1882, m. 20 May 1867 in Knoxville IL) — they had <strong>6 sons and 4 daughters</strong>; Elnora died of complications from childbirth shortly after their son Warren was born. Three of those children died in infancy and are buried beside her in Robbins Cemetery: <strong>James Clyde Gaddis</strong> (b. 21 Feb 1876, d. 8 Aug 1876), <strong>Warren Phelps Gaddis</strong> (b. 5 Mar 1882, d. 8 Sep 1882, born ~3 months before his mother died), and another \"Clyde.\" One likely surviving eldest was <strong>Harry Bluford Gaddis</strong> (b. 2 Jun 1875, d. 14 Jan 1949, buried Lincoln, IL — Find-a-Grave #148707160). His <strong>fifth child</strong> <strong>Albert Watts Gaddis</strong> carries the line that Lloyd A. Gaddis (Jason's contemporary cousin, of Seattle) descends from. Second marriage on <strong>17 February 1887</strong> to <strong>Della Lyon</strong> (recorded as \"Adele\" or \"Emma Adele\" in family memory, b. 1856 d. 1939) — they had <strong>1 son and 2 daughters</strong>, the son being Harry Lloyd Gaddis Sr. Died <strong>2 February 1904</strong> in Wataga; buried in <strong>Robbins Cemetery, Sparta Township, Knox County, IL</strong>; the headstone reads \"FATHER\" with the inscription <em>\"Lucas Gaddis, son of John and Chloe (Fox) Gaddis.\"</em>",
      "sources": "<a href=\"https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/66049955/lucas-gaddis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Find-a-Grave memorial #66049955</a> (Patty Gaddis, contributor); <a href=\"https://knox.illinoisgenweb.org/marriagesg.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Knox County IL Marriage Index</a> (entries for both marriages); RootsWeb gaddis2 page (Julie Gaddis, contributor).",
