      "nm": "Louis \"Lou\" Hicks",
      "ep": "Denver vegetable cart · stock investor",
      "dt": "Adopted 26 Jan 1884 (E. St. Louis, IL) – d. 22 Feb 1976 (Denver, CO)",
      "rel": "2nd-great-grandfather (Thelma's father, on the Hicks side)",
      "notes": "<strong>Adopted on 26 January 1884 in East St. Louis, Clair County, Illinois</strong> — the chart explicitly notes \"(Adopted)\" beside his date. Adoptive parents: <strong>Alvin E. Hicks</strong> (b. 1854, Rosita, Custer Co. CO) and <strong>Rosita Nold</strong> (b. 1864 d. 1926, originally from St. Louis MO). Birth parents unknown. Married <strong>Ethel Agnes Thompson</strong> (b. 17 Nov 1893, d. 22 Feb 1976, Denver) — their <strong>50th wedding anniversary in 1958</strong> is preserved in a family photo. Spent the Great Depression years selling vegetables from a cart he hauled around Denver; eventually started buying and selling stock and did very well at it. Their <strong>seven children</strong> were <strong>Louise Elisabeth \"LuLu,\" Thelma Grace</strong> (our ancestor), <strong>Robert Roscoe \"Bob,\" Mildred R. \"Mary,\" Sarah Mae, Bruce M.</strong> and <strong>Paul D. \"Jack.\"</strong> The arc — Depression-era street vendor turned self-taught stock investor — is a quintessentially American story that doesn't make it into census records. (<em>Tad Olsen</em>, mentioned by Geoff Gaddis as \"the son of one of my cousins,\" ties at least one Hicks cousin branch into the Olsen surname.)",
      "sources": "Hicks Cousins Family History chart (compiled c. 2020s, family record); Louis & Ethel Hicks 1958 photograph; Geoffrey Beach Gaddis family-line note (May 2026).",
